## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to reason about data deletion in log-based, multi-tenant systems; covers tombstone propagation, privacy guarantees, auditability, and verification under failure conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Tombstone semantics, cross-topic propagation, and per-tenant isolation
- Idempotence, retention versus deletion semantics
- Audit trails and verification under failure conditions
- Handling cross-topic joins while preserving deletion markers
- Compression-aware deletion propagation

## Code Example
```javascript
function createTombstone(tenantId, key, deletionTimestamp) {
  return {
    tenantId,
    key,
    deletionTimestamp,
    isTombstone: true
  };
}

function propagateTombstone(tombstone, pipeline) {
  pipeline.forEach(stage => {
    stage.emit(tombstone);
    stage.compress(); // Preserve tombstone through compression
  });
}
```

Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical deletion workflows in multi-tenant KCNA, balancing legal rights, data minimization, and system integrity. It requires concrete mappings, key management, tombstone strategies, and test plans that prove irreversibility while keeping performance acceptable.

Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped data maps and identifiers
- Per-tenant key rotation and re-encryption
- Tombstones, garbage collection, and index scrubbing
- Auditing, proofs, and compliance testing
- Backups, restores, and consistency guarantees

Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-implementation skeleton
function eraseTenantData(tenantId) {
  revokeAccess(tenantId);
  rotateKeys(tenantId);
  scrubIngest(tenantId);
  scrubEnrichment(tenantId);
  scrubAnalytics(tenantId);
  publishTombstones(tenantId);
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for complete deletion in a live cluster?
- How do backups interact with the deletion policy?
